@@ -0,0 +1,141 @@
+# AI-DLC on Coding Harness
+
+The `harness` runtime is the framework's distribution for **Coding Harness**
+(the `harness` CLI). One deterministic core, many harnesses: the engine, state
+machine, audit log, graph, swarm referee, and learnings gate are byte-identical
+across every distribution — only the shell differs. The source/development tree
+is **generated** into ignored local `dist/harness/` from `core/` +
+`harness/harness/` by `bun scripts/package.ts harness`; never hand-edit it.
+
+Harness-specific onboarding lives in `.harness-aidlc/onboarding.md`; the AI-DLC
+extension injects it, with the method files, into every session's system
+prompt. The root `AGENTS.md` block is harness-neutral and shared with the other
+installed harnesses.
+
+## Layout: one extension file, one engine directory
+
+`.harness/` is your own Coding Harness configuration, so AI-DLC places a single
+file there and keeps everything else in its own directory:
+
+- **`.harness/extensions/aidlc.ts`** — the bootstrap Coding Harness auto-loads
+  from the project. It only re-exports the adapter, so it never changes between
+  releases. AI-DLC manages it as a whole file.
+- **`.harness-aidlc/`** — the engine tree (tools, hooks, skills, agents,
+  knowledge, scopes, sensors, aidlc-common) plus the host shell in
+  `.harness-aidlc/host/`:
+  - `adapter.ts` — the extension: it maps harness events onto the core hooks
+    and contributes the distribution's resources through `resources_discover`;
+  - `agents/` — the 14 personas as task agents;
+  - `prompts/aidlc.md` — the `/aidlc` prompt;
+  - `includes.json` — the method files the adapter injects into the system
+    prompt (`aidlc/spaces/<active-space>/memory/`, re-pointed on a space switch).
+
+Coding Harness skips gitignored extension files, so the bootstrap must stay
+tracked; `aidlc doctor` checks it.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- **Coding Harness ≥ 1.1.0** — the release that fires `resources_discover` and
+  accepts extension-contributed agent, rule and watchdog locations. Check with
+  `harness --version`.
+- **bun** only when generating or running the source/development `dist/`
+  projection. Native installs dispatch through the installed `aidlc` executable.
+- **A model provider** — model access belongs to Coding Harness. The shipped
+  task agents pin no model; balanced-tier agents (the reviewers) carry
+  `thinkingLevel: medium`, everything else inherits the session settings.
+
+## Install
+
+### Native channel (recommended)
+
+With an `aidlc` build that includes the `harness` distribution installed:
+
+```bash
+cd your-project
+aidlc config --harness harness
+aidlc doctor
+harness
+```
+
+Then type `/aidlc` (or describe what you want to build).
+
+### Manual copy (alternative)
+
+Copy the complete `runtime/harness/` directory from a release's
+`aidlc-copy-runtime-X.Y.Z.tar.gz` (or a source checkout's generated
+`dist/harness/`) into the project root, merging `.gitignore` and `AGENTS.md`.
+This channel needs bun on the hook path; the adapter invokes
+`bun .harness-aidlc/tools/aidlc.ts`.
+
+## How the hooks run
+
+Coding Harness has no hook configuration file; extensions subscribe to events
+in-process. The adapter runs each core hook through the AI-DLC dispatcher
+(`aidlc engine hook <name>`) with the same input Claude Code hooks receive:
+
+| Harness event | Core hook |
+|---|---|
+| `session_start`, `session_shutdown` | `session-start`, `session-end` |
+| `input` (interactive or RPC) | `record-human-turn` |
+| `tool_call` | `fold-usage`; `deliver-stage-rules` and `plan-approval-guard` for `task`; `state-transition-guard`, `reviewer-scope` (subagents), `review-freeze`, `plan-approval-guard` for file and shell tools |
+| `tool_result` | `write-audit-log` and `run-sensors` for writes, `sync-workflow-state` for todos, `record-human-turn` for answered `ask` calls, `rebuild-stage-graph` for bash, `log-subagent` for tasks, `fold-usage` |
+| `session_stop` | `continue-workflow` (a block becomes a continuation turn) |
+| `session_before_compact` | `validate-state` |
+| `turn_end`, `agent_end` | status line |
+
+A hook's exit 2 blocks the tool call with the hook's reason; any other failure
+fails open, as on the other harnesses.
+
+## What's different on Coding Harness
+
+- **Structured questions use the `ask` tool.** The adapter removes a
+  `recommended` option from every `ask` call, so no human decision opens with
+  an option pre-selected. An answer the `ask` timeout selected is not a human
+  decision: it records no human presence and the conductor re-asks.
+- **Sensor results reach the model.** After a write, the sensors' verdicts
+  (failures with their detail excerpt) reach the model as context right after
+  the tool result, so it fixes the artifact in the same turn. Set
+  `AIDLC_HARNESS_SENSOR_FEEDBACK=0` to turn this off; the audit log is the same
+  either way.
+- **Method files are injected, not imported.** The adapter appends the
+  onboarding and the method files from `host/includes.json` to the system prompt
+  of every turn; the text only changes when a file does, so the prompt cache holds.
+- **No project command allowlist.** Tool approval follows your Coding Harness
+  settings. The adapter still refuses a chained or redirected framework command:
+  it allows one direct `aidlc …` (or shipped `bun .harness-aidlc/…`) invocation.
+- **Usage.** The usage ledger reads harness session transcripts, including
+  subagent and advisor transcripts in the session's artifacts directory, and
+  keeps the cost Coding Harness reported for models the AI-DLC rate table lacks.
+- **Plugins** compose the way they do on opencode: build the plugin for the
+  `harness` target, then run `PLUGIN_ROOT=<built plugin> aidlc plugin sync`.
+  Plugin personas land in `.harness-aidlc/host/agents/` as task agents.
+
+## Alongside Claude Code
+
+One project can carry both distributions: run `aidlc config --harness claude`
+and `aidlc config --harness harness` in either order. Each host runs AI-DLC from
+its own tree and both share the workflow under `aidlc/`.
+
+- **`/aidlc` is the harness skill.** Coding Harness also reads `.claude/skills`;
+  from version 1.1.0 a skill an extension contributes replaces another tool's
+  skill of the same name, so the `aidlc` skill here is this distribution's.
+- **Framework commands resolve this tree.** The adapter sets
+  `AIDLC_HARNESS_DIR=.harness-aidlc` for the commands a session runs, so
+  `aidlc …` acts on `.harness-aidlc/`, and it refuses `bun .claude/tools/…`
+  with the path to use instead.
+- **Project context.** Coding Harness prefers `.claude/CLAUDE.md` over
+  `AGENTS.md` as the project's context file. Its Claude-specific notes reach the
+  model, but the method itself arrives through the adapter either way.
+- **Doctor.** Each tree's `aidlc doctor` checks its own wiring and names it in
+  the Project heading.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- `aidlc doctor` names every missing piece: the bootstrap, the adapter, the
+  `/aidlc` prompt, the task agents, the include list, and a gitignore rule that
+  hides the bootstrap.
+- `AIDLC_HARNESS_DEBUG=1` logs every hook run (hook, exit code, duration) to
+  `$XDG_STATE_HOME/aidlc-harness/<project hash>.log`
+  (`~/.local/state/aidlc-harness/` by default).
+- After `aidlc update`, run `aidlc doctor` and refresh the project with
+  `aidlc config` between workflows.
